window.docContents[199].resolve({"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Reasoning-About-Lists--Induction-on-Lists--Generalizing-Statements":{"contents":"\n\nSometimes statements need to be generalized to prove them\nby induction:\n\nexample (c n : Nat) :\n    myRepeat n c ++ myRepeat n c = myRepeat n (c + c) := by\n  induction c with\n  | zero => rw [repeat_zero, nil_append]\n  | succ c' ih =>\n    rw [repeat_succ]\n    -- Now we seem to be stuck.  The IH only works for c' + c',\n    -- but we need c' + 1 + (c' + 1).\n    sorry\n\n\n\n\nA generalization that gives a stronger inductive hypothesis:\n\ntheorem myRepeat_plus (c1 c2 n : Nat) :\n    myRepeat n c1 ++ myRepeat n c2 = myRepeat n (c1 + c2) := by\n  induction c1 with\n  | zero =>\n    rw [repeat_zero, Nat.zero_add, nil_append]\n  | succ c1' ih =>\n    rw [Nat.succ_add, repeat_succ, repeat_succ, cons_append, ih]\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Lists: Working with Structured Data\u0009Reasoning About Lists\u0009Induction on Lists","header":"4.3.1.1. Generalizing Statements","id":"/Lists/#Logical-Foundations--Lists___-Working-with-Structured-Data--Reasoning-About-Lists--Induction-on-Lists--Generalizing-Statements"}});